WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:22.065
If you look at Hollywood, there's these
companies that have been there for

00:00:22.065 --> 00:00:24.155
a long time, Fox, Universal, Disney,

00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:27.285
they've been making entertainment
product for a long time.

00:00:27.285 --> 00:00:31.141
And now you have in Silicon Valley
companies that have been around for

00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:34.666
a very short period of time,
the Facebooks, the Instagrams,

00:00:34.666 --> 00:00:39.277
the Snapchats, and the question is, where
are we going to be in the decades to come?

00:00:39.277 --> 00:00:42.733
Are those studios still going to be out
there making films the way they've been

00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:43.765
doing for a long time?

00:00:43.765 --> 00:00:46.961
And will these new companies be
continuing what they're going to do, or

00:00:46.961 --> 00:00:48.921
are they going to morph
into something else?

00:00:48.921 --> 00:00:53.097
[MUSIC]

00:00:53.097 --> 00:00:57.361
The technology is amazing and
people love it and they love iMacs and

00:00:57.361 --> 00:01:01.561
they love VR and they love all
the stuff that is coming along next.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:05.998
However, people watch movies on their
iPhones, and if you're making a film and

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:10.629
you spend all this time working on it, and
someone's looking at in on an iPhone, you

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:15.195
feel like, well, jeez, I spent all this
time like worrying about the lighting and

00:01:15.195 --> 00:01:19.632
the camera move and they're watching it
on an iPhone, but that's the reality of

00:01:19.632 --> 00:01:23.780
the world that we're in, and that's
why I think story is still king here.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.317
I watched this IMAX film
about climbing up Everest and

00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:32.046
it was filmed spectacularly well,
and I thought it was pretty boring.

00:01:32.046 --> 00:01:36.406
And then there's a scene there that's
amazingly powerful where somebody says

00:01:36.406 --> 00:01:39.984
goodbye to their wife,
that they're dying on the mountain, and

00:01:39.984 --> 00:01:43.950
it was filmed with this very cheap camera,
and that's what was moving,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.118
that's what made the film memorable.

00:01:46.118 --> 00:01:50.677
[MUSIC]

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:56.397
The thing to remember about Silicon Valley
is that technology is everywhere here.

00:01:56.397 --> 00:01:59.244
The, sort of the joke is if you're
not paying for the product,

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:00.228
you are the product.

00:02:00.228 --> 00:02:05.675
And so, there's an amazing amount of
data that's out there about what you do,

00:02:05.675 --> 00:02:09.743
but there are places where data
can't really penetrate and

00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:14.242
part of what makes films films is
you can't just look at the data.

00:02:14.242 --> 00:02:17.597
If someone could have figured out
a formula for predicting movie success,

00:02:17.597 --> 00:02:20.699
they would have done it a long time ago,
it would have been game over.

00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:25.687
There still is something that comes
from the heart and the brain, and

00:02:25.687 --> 00:02:31.019
that you try to come up with something
creative that engages an audience,

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:34.287
and wants them to hang in with your story,
and

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:39.361
wants them to forget about the sort
of the soccer game where they lost,

00:02:39.361 --> 00:02:44.951
or the fact that their knee hurts, or
they gotta rush home to the babysitter,

00:02:44.951 --> 00:02:48.563
that they get swept into
the part of the story, and

00:02:48.563 --> 00:02:53.566
when you make a film, there's some
really rewarding parts of that.

00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:58.469
I mean, there's moments when you're making
a film where you feel like the information

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:01.384
you're seeing is powerful and
it's touching, and

00:03:01.384 --> 00:03:05.229
you hope that you have the skills,
and the technical abilities, and

00:03:05.229 --> 00:03:08.227
the creative abilities to
bring that to an audience.

00:03:08.227 --> 00:03:12.358
And if you're affected when you're doing
an interview in a documentary film, and

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:16.429
you look around and your entire crew is
crying, that they're moved by story, and

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:20.146
you're crying as well because someone's
telling such a powerful story,

00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:23.509
you say to yourself, my God,
I hope that one day a movie theater full

00:03:23.509 --> 00:03:27.432
of people will be just as affected, or
they'll watch it on TV and be affected.

00:03:27.432 --> 00:03:32.125
And that is one-to-one, personal
storytelling, where the filmmaker's

00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:36.599
trying to make a connection with
the audience, but then you hope it goes to

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:41.015
a wide audience and that people will
also have an emotional connection.

00:03:41.015 --> 00:03:44.748
[MUSIC]

00:03:44.748 --> 00:03:48.908
We are in a situation now where we had
a film at Sundance that came out this year

00:03:48.908 --> 00:03:53.263
that was done all on the iPhone, the Oscar
winning documentary, Searching for

00:03:53.263 --> 00:03:56.188
Sugarman, was shot partially on an iPhone,
I mean,

00:03:56.188 --> 00:03:59.958
audiences have shown this kind of
willingness to embrace things shot

00:03:59.958 --> 00:04:02.637
on cheap technology if
the story is compelling.

00:04:02.637 --> 00:04:05.752
What does it make you start watching
at minute one and go to minute five and

00:04:05.752 --> 00:04:06.888
stay all the way in there?

00:04:06.888 --> 00:04:09.818
You need a compelling story
with people you care about.

00:04:09.818 --> 00:04:14.158
It used to be in movies that you could
sort of trick people into going to see

00:04:14.158 --> 00:04:14.738
a movie.

00:04:14.738 --> 00:04:18.623
So you could spend lavishly
on newspaper and TV ads, and

00:04:18.623 --> 00:04:22.179
by the time people realized
the film was terrible,

00:04:22.179 --> 00:04:25.997
it was already a hit, well,
now it's hard to do that.

00:04:25.997 --> 00:04:31.649
Now, people go into theaters, the movie
doesn't seem that good, on Friday night,

00:04:31.649 --> 00:04:36.517
they're busy sending texts or going
out on Facebook, and I think it hurts

00:04:36.517 --> 00:04:41.403
over publicized bad movies but
also helps under publicized good movies.

00:04:41.403 --> 00:04:44.537
[MUSIC]

00:04:44.537 --> 00:04:48.375
So if you look at Pixar,
that is a beautiful marriage because it's

00:04:48.375 --> 00:04:53.310
technologically sophisticating, great,
and the storytelling is first rate, and

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.009
the way they work together is in
a highly collaborative way, and

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.498
that's everything working really well.

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:04.103
And so I think you're going to see more of
these examples coming forward as people

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:05.861
sort of admire the Pixar way.

00:05:05.861 --> 00:05:10.578
And then so I think that, inevitably,
the two sides are going to become closer.

00:05:10.578 --> 00:05:14.673
It's my stong belief that this is a
marriage of Silicon Valley and Hollywood,

00:05:14.673 --> 00:05:17.156
sometimes they're
suspicious of each other,

00:05:17.156 --> 00:05:21.580
sometimes they don't quite understand each
other, but we really need each other.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:26.021
I mean, we're sitting right now
in the heart of Silicon Valley,

00:05:26.021 --> 00:05:31.217
it's where products are designed,
it's where the cutting edge truly is.

00:05:31.217 --> 00:05:35.958
On the other hand, when you're watching on
your iPhone, or your iPad, or your laptop,

00:05:35.958 --> 00:05:39.498
or however you're watching,
there's gotta be content on that,

00:05:39.498 --> 00:05:43.737
and there's not that much content that's
coming out of northern California,

00:05:43.737 --> 00:05:47.217
the content is coming primarily
out of southern California, and

00:05:47.217 --> 00:05:49.588
those are stories that
have to be well told.

00:05:49.588 --> 00:05:54.869
And I think there's this idea that both
sides can learn from the other side,

00:05:54.869 --> 00:06:00.316
and I think that with this marriage,
you're going to see increasingly better,

00:06:00.316 --> 00:06:05.351
better shows, better delivered,
especially as an audience has come to

00:06:05.351 --> 00:06:10.797
embrace this idea that audiences just
want good content and they don't really

00:06:10.797 --> 00:06:15.868
care where it's coming from,
they will go to where the content is good.

00:06:15.868 --> 00:06:17.739
[MUSIC]

